        "head_faq": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "",
            "title": "The questions people ask about the stack.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "faq1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need all five?",
            "answer": "<p>No. The list is ranked for a reason - start at the top and add downward only if there is a gap. Most people are covered by the first two or three, and the B vitamins, zinc, copper and CoQ10 that would otherwise be separate purchases are already inside the MUTO formula.</p>",
            "open_by_default": true
          }
        },
        "faq2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why nicotinamide riboside rather than NMN?",
            "answer": "<p>NR has the larger body of published human research behind it, which is why it is the base of the formula at 300mg. The more important difference is what sits alongside it: pterostilbene extends how long raised NAD⁺ levels last, and BioPerine® improves absorption. A single-ingredient NAD⁺ product leaves that work undone.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Where does this fit if I already take a stack?",
            "answer": "<p>Most of a stack works on inputs the body then has to process. MUTO works a layer beneath that, on the cell’s capacity to use any of it. Doses are printed in full on the label, so overlap is visible rather than guessed at - the usual outcome is that it replaces two or three separate products rather than adding to them.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How soon will I feel something?",
            "answer": "<p>There are no stimulants in it, so nothing in the first hour. Most people report the afternoon dip going in weeks three to four, and wearable data typically moves between day 30 and day 60. Read it as a trend across weeks, not a single morning’s reading.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        }
